sPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-visitors-can-smell-incoherence</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 22 Jul 2026 11:03:42 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/aa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1068727,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;Two children sitting on a mountain hillside, gazing up at the night sky where faint, almost invisible stars are drowned out by a multitude of glowing orange and white streetlights all around them, melancholic atmosphere, slight mist in the air, intense artificial lights creating flares, halos and reflections on the ground&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/207135100?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="Two children sitting on a mountain hillside, gazing up at the night sky where faint, almost invisible stars are drowned out by a multitude of glowing orange and white streetlights all around them, melancholic atmosphere, slight mist in the air, intense artificial lights creating flares, halos and reflections on the ground" title="Two children sitting on a mountain hillside, gazing up at the night sky where faint, almost invisible stars are drowned out by a multitude of glowing orange and white streetlights all around them, melancholic atmosphere, slight mist in the air, intense artificial lights creating flares, halos and reflections on the ground" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!DoxE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Faa9baf08-95dc-4b17-9dc5-d21771b421c8_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>A visitor who walks into your museum for a specific exhibition expects every corner of the building to speak that language. The signage. The staff. The lighting. The sounds. Every detail either reinforces the promise you made, or quietly betrays it.</p><p>This isn&#8217;t a nice-to-have. It&#8217;s the single biggest factor in whether someone walks out thinking &#8220;I need to tell my friends&#8221; or &#8220;well, that was fine&#8221;.</p><p>The data backs this up with uncomfortable clarity. The AAM/Wilkening 2024 Annual Survey of Museum-Goers (90,178 respondents across 202 museums) found that visitors who feel staff &#8220;always make me feel welcome&#8221; visit more frequently, are far more likely to recommend the museum, and rate it higher on almost every dimension. But here&#8217;s the number that should keep you up at night: the 3% who reported negative staff experiences were 29 times more likely to say the museum &#8220;doesn&#8217;t do anything well.&#8221; Not 29% more likely. Twenty-nine times.</p><p>One broken touchpoint doesn&#8217;t just weaken the experience. It poisons everything around it.</p><h2>The camping test</h2><p>Last weekend I took my kids camping. I&#8217;d spent the whole drive up getting them excited about astronomy. The place was perfect on paper: bungalows shaped like observatories, perched on a mountaintop, with an actual observatory on-site. Everything about their marketing said &#8220;come here and watch the stars&#8221;.</p><p>Then we arrived.</p><p>The owner, genuinely kind and welcoming, couldn&#8217;t answer my basic question about which constellations were visible from up there. He just wasn&#8217;t very informed on the topic. The observatory turned out to be closed and abandoned. And from sunset to sunrise, artificial lights scattered everywhere made the stars less visible than they are from my city apartment.</p><p>My kids didn&#8217;t complain. They found other things to enjoy. But I noticed the gap between what was promised and what was delivered. And that gap is exactly what your visitors notice too, whether they tell you or not.</p><p>Manifesto&#8217;s 2025 &#8220;Curating Connection&#8221; study (2,000 UK visitors, 300 cultural leaders) puts a number on this silence: only 30% of visitors stay engaged after a visit, and only when their expectations were exceeded. When expectations are merely met, or worse, contradicted, disengagement is silent. People don&#8217;t write angry emails. They simply don&#8217;t come back.</p><h2>The coherence equation</h2><p>Pine &amp; Gilmore&#8217;s Experience Economy framework, now over 25 years old, still offers the clearest operating principle for this: eliminate negative cues. Anything that doesn&#8217;t contribute to the theme is a negative cue. Not neutral. Negative. Because it breaks the spell.</p><p>That camping site had a strong theme. Astronomy. Mountaintop location. Observatory-shaped bungalows. But the execution leaked at every joint. The owner&#8217;s knowledge, the closed observatory, the light pollution. Each one was a negative cue that contradicted the story the place was trying to tell.</p><p>Cultural institutions do the same thing constantly. You spend months curating an exhibition, then the front desk staff can&#8217;t describe what&#8217;s on display. Your website promises an immersive journey, but the first thing visitors encounter is a confusing ticket queue. Your audioguide covers the main collection beautifully, but the temporary exhibition has no audio content at all.</p><p>The accesso Voice of the Visitor 2026 report (2.5 million reviews across 500 attractions in 37 countries) found that when visitors praise staff friendliness in a 5-star review, it&#8217;s positive 98.6% of the time. Good news. But when staff professionalism fails, it&#8217;s negative 97.9% of the time it&#8217;s mentioned, and reviews with negative professionalism average just 1.90 stars, the lowest of any topic in the dataset. This asymmetry matters: a great staff interaction can elevate the whole visit, but a bad one doesn&#8217;t just lower the score. It reframes everything else as worse.</p><h2>Your visitors are comparing you to everything</h2><p>Here&#8217;s the uncomfortable context. The US immersive experience market reached $3.9 billion in 2024, up from $1.5 billion in 2019, an average annual growth rate of 21% (according to Habo&#8217;s market analysis reported by Blooloop). Your visitors aren&#8217;t just comparing your museum to other museums. They&#8217;re comparing it to every immersive, themed, carefully designed experience they&#8217;ve had in the last year. Escape rooms. Immersive art shows. Even well-designed retail stores.</p><p>Manifesto&#8217;s research makes this explicit: audiences now benchmark cultural experiences against Netflix, immersive pop-ups, TikTok, and YouTube. The expectations are set outside your sector, and they walk through your door carrying them.</p><p>This doesn&#8217;t mean you need a $3 million projection room. It means every touchpoint, from the parking lot to the exit survey, needs to tell the same story. The small details compound.</p><h2>The coherence checklist</h2><p>Here&#8217;s a practical test you can run next Monday morning. Walk through your own institution as a first-time visitor. Start from the moment you see the building from outside.</p><p><strong>Does your staff pass the &#8220;curious visitor&#8221; test?</strong> Ask every front-facing team member one genuine question about the current exhibition. Not a quiz. Just: &#8220;What&#8217;s your favorite piece in this show?&#8221; or &#8220;What surprises most visitors?&#8221;. If they hesitate, you have a coherence gap. The fix isn&#8217;t a manual. It&#8217;s giving your team time to experience the exhibition themselves, listen to the audioguide, and form their own relationship with the content before opening day.</p><p><strong>Does every physical detail reinforce or contradict your theme?</strong> Walk the full visitor path. Note every moment where something pulls you out of the story. The vending machine next to the Renaissance gallery. The fluorescent lighting in the immersive room. The generic background music that has nothing to do with the exhibition. Each one is a negative cue that costs you nothing to fix, but everything to ignore.</p><p><strong>Does your post-visit experience match the quality of the visit itself?</strong> Manifesto found that 37% of all museum visits come from recommendations and return visits, more than search, paid media, or social channels. So what does your visitor take home? A memory? A photo opportunity? A reason to text a friend? Or just a ticket stub?</p><p>The camping site got almost everything right in their marketing. They understood the theme, they designed the structures around it, they picked the perfect location. But they forgot to extend that coherence to the people and the details. The owner who couldn&#8217;t talk about stars. The observatory that was closed. The lights that killed the sky.</p><p><strong>Your visitors arrive hoping to be immersed in your world. Don&#8217;t give them reasons to notice where the world ends and the real world begins.</strong></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Your Hidden Competitor Is Not Another Museum]]></title><description><![CDATA[How to serve the solitary visitor who uses your museum as a wellness retreat]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-hidden-competitor-is-not-another</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-hidden-competitor-is-not-another</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 15 Jul 2026 11:04:20 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://subs