1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1262227,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/206023520?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9fcca868-0c0d-401e-b4c1-715e3d1239c3_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GJ0k!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9fcca868-0c0d-401e-b4c1-715e3d1239c3_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GJ0k!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9fcca868-0c0d-401e-b4c1-715e3d1239c3_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GJ0k!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9fcca868-0c0d-401e-b4c1-715e3d1239c3_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GJ0k!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9fcca868-0c0d-401e-b4c1-715e3d1239c3_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Your most underestimated competitor is not the museum across town. It&#8217;s the spa down the road, the meditation retreat, the mindfulness app. There is a visitor who walks into your spaces not looking for culture. They are looking for peace. And if you are not designing for them, you are losing them without knowing it.</p><p>I speak from direct experience. There are moments when my energy drops, my mind is overloaded and creativity stalls. In those moments I don&#8217;t seek distraction. I seek restoration. <strong>And the place where I find it is not a wellness center. It&#8217;s a museum.</strong></p><p>I schedule regular visits to cultural spaces, not always because of the exhibition theme. Sometimes the topic matters less than the visit itself. I move through it like a mindfulness session: I walk in, I slow down, and something in the rhythm of my breathing changes. I leave with ideas I didn&#8217;t have when I arrived, with notes connecting what I saw to my work, with energy for the next meeting at the office.</p><p>This is not unusual behavior. It is a real, measurable visitor segment, and it&#8217;s larger than you might think.</p><h3>Who is the solitary visitor</h3><p>The AAM/Wilkening Annual Survey of 2025, the largest survey of American museum-goers (98,904 respondents, 202 museums), found that about 15% of visitors prefer to visit alone. That may not sound like a huge number, but the more interesting finding lies elsewhere.</p><p>A study at the Belvedere in Vienna, published in Museum Management and Curatorship in 2024, tracked the behavior of visitor pairs using a dedicated app called Visitracker. The result: the ratio of solitary to shared interactions was 7 to 3. In other words, out of every 10 observed interactions with artworks, 7 happen in solitude, even when the visitor arrived with someone else.</p><p><strong>In practice, the solitary experience is not just about those who arrive alone. It concerns almost everyone, once they cross the threshold.</strong></p><p>The visitor I&#8217;m describing falls in love with the space before the artworks. The right temperature, perhaps gentle background music at the entrance echoing the exhibition theme, or the silence the space emanates that is already magical on its own. They want to be alone, they wish they could have certain exhibition rooms all to themselves, sit in front of a painting and admire it without rush. With the right information at their fingertips.</p><p>John Falk, in his book Identity and the Museum Visitor Experience (2009), identified five identity-related motivations for museum visits. One of these is the &#8220;Recharger&#8221;, the person who uses the museum as a refuge, a place of inner restoration. Someone seeking, in Falk&#8217;s words, &#8220;a contemplative or restorative experience&#8221;. The size of this segment varies enormously from institution to institution. At a science center it may be marginal, because the environment is designed for active interaction. At a botanical garden or an art gallery with contemplative spaces, it can become the dominant segment. The point is not the exact percentage. It&#8217;s recognizing that this motivation exists and that certain spaces attract it naturally more than others.</p><h3>27 seconds</h3><p>There is a number worth knowing. In 2001, Jeffrey and Lisa Smith observed 150 visitors at the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York and measured how long they spent in front of six masterpieces. Average time: 27.2 seconds. Median: 17 seconds.</p><p>Subsequent studies have confirmed that this number has not changed in 25 years, despite smartphones (or perhaps because of them). Slow Art Day, a global movement born in 2008 from an experiment at the Jewish Museum in New York, asks participants to dedicate 5 to 10 minutes per work. In 15 years, over 1,500 venues across all seven continents have participated, with about 240 locations in 2026 alone.</p><p><strong>The gap between 27 seconds and 10 minutes is enormous. And the solitary visitor I&#8217;m describing is exactly the one who would fill that gap.</strong> They will listen to every track of the audio guide. They want to go deeper. They take notes. Notes to remember that moment, notes that connect the story they are hearing to their work.</p><h3>The clinical data backs it up</h3><p>It&#8217;s not just a feeling. A study by King&#8217;s College London conducted at the Courtauld Gallery in 2025 monitored 50 volunteers during 20-minute sessions in front of works by Manet, Van Gogh and Gauguin. Those viewing original artworks showed an average cortisol reduction of 22%. In the control group, which viewed reproductions of the same works in a neutral environment, no significant reduction was observed. The study has not yet been peer-reviewed, but the finding is remarkable: it&#8217;s not just that art reduces stress. The context, the original, the physical space make the difference.</p><p>In Montreal, the Museum of Fine Arts took this concept a step further. In a study published in Frontiers in Medicine in 2025, they documented that a single museum visit prescribed by a physician produces statistically significant improvements in patients&#8217; mental wellbeing and quality of life. The &#8220;museum on prescription&#8221; program has been active since 2018, and similar initiatives now exist in the United Kingdom (where social prescribing is integrated into the national health system), in Belgium and in France.</p><p><strong>Museums don&#8217;t just compete with other museums for visitors&#8217; attention. They compete with the wellness industry</strong>, a sector growing at double digits where tourists spend on average 41% more than the typical tourist (Global Wellness Institute, 2024). This doesn&#8217;t mean turning the museum into a spa. But ignoring that a portion of your visitors chooses you with the same motivation they would choose a retreat means not understanding your own market.</p><h3>What you can do, concretely</h3><p>I&#8217;m not suggesting you transform the museum into a wellness center. I&#8217;m suggesting you recognize that some visitors already choose you with that motivation, and design for them intentionally.</p><p><strong>Consider special sessions with genuinely reduced capacity.</strong> Not every day, but one morning a month, an early opening, a dedicated evening. The British Museum offers volunteer-led out-of-hours tours at &#163;35 per person, in a museum that is normally free. MoMA in New York requires a minimum of $1,100 for private tours before opening hours. These pricing models work because they are built on a simple principle: fewer people, more space, higher perceived value.</p><p>I know, you immediately think about the bottom line. Fewer tickets in the same time slot, all services running at full capacity. But your solitary visitor is not a passerby. They gifted themselves this visit instead of going to a spa. They&#8217;re in no hurry. They will savor a quality pastry at your caf&#233; while browsing the brochures you left for them, or install the audio guide on their phone with all the time in the world. Maybe they couldn&#8217;t prepare anything at home because their life is hectic, but once inside, they&#8217;re all yours.</p><p><strong>Run a sensory audit of your spaces.</strong> In a context like this, how much do sounds, smells, and lights matter? Enormously. Have you ever measured your spaces at different times of the day, mapping how sensory conditions change across time slots? An open window, a speaker to reposition, a spotlight to dim. This analysis should be done across different seasons, because conditions are never the same. It costs almost nothing, but it can radically change the experience.</p><p><strong>Schedule slow looking sessions.</strong> Cost: practically zero. Impact: extremely high for this segment. Slow Art Day proves the format works and there is demand. You don&#8217;t need to wait for April, a monthly slow observation session, with 5 selected works and 10 minutes each, is an offering that differentiates and that this visitor will actively seek out.</p><p><strong>Invest in the welcome moment.</strong> Think about being able to exchange a couple of personalized words with the visitor about the journey they are about to take, without the rush of greeting the next one. For the solitary visitor, those two minutes of exclusive attention are worth more than any brochure.</p><h3>Not the hardest audience to please</h3><p>There is a paradox here. This visitor seems demanding because they seek an intimate experience, but they are actually the easiest to satisfy. If they come to you to relax, it&#8217;s because outside they are already loaded with stress and noise. It doesn&#8217;t take much to make them feel better. This doesn&#8217;t mean underestimating how to design the experience. It means focusing on aspects tied more to sensation than to spectacle.</p><p><strong>This is a visitor who comes looking for an inner peace that makes them feel creative. Inspire them.</strong></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The mission your visitors never read, but always feel]]></title><description><![CDATA[90% of American art museums have rewritten their mission around people, not objects. What about you?]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-mission-your-visitors-never-read</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-mission-your-visitors-never-read</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 11:02:42 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/f04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1427698,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;People inside a Starbucks focused on drinking coffee with open laptops, in a modern and cozy atmosphere, natural daylight streaming through large windows. View from the street looking into the wide caf&#233; window, reflections of cars and pedestrians on the glass, warm interior with wooden tables&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/204409665?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="People inside a Starbucks focused on drinking coffee with open laptops, in a modern and cozy atmosphere, natural daylight streaming through large windows. View from the street looking into the wide caf&#233; window, reflections of cars and pedestrians on the glass, warm interior with wooden tables" title="People inside a Starbucks focused on drinking coffee with open laptops, in a modern and cozy atmosphere, natural daylight streaming through large windows. View from the street looking into the wide caf&#233; window, reflections of cars and pedestrians on the glass, warm interior with wooden tables" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!LWmq!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Ff04f3bec-85f9-4953-98a7-eae09059a392_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Do you have a written mission? Most likely, yes. But try asking yourself this question: <strong>does your mission describe what you preserve or how you transform the people who walk through your doors?</strong></p><p>This is not a nuance. A 2024 study by Remuseum analyzed the mission statements of all 199 institutions in the Association of Art Museum Directors, and the finding is striking: nearly 90% of American art museums now define their mission in terms of serving the public. Only 11% still put objects at the center. In just a few decades, the sector has flipped, moving from being &#8220;about something&#8221; to being &#8220;for somebody.&#8221;</p><p><strong>Yet having a people-centered mission is not enough. Every touchpoint needs to breathe it.</strong></p><p>A few months ago I walked past a Starbucks and saw it full of people working, laptops open. It took me back twenty years, to my work placement period in Vancouver. Those coffee shops were my comfort zone, where I felt inspired to open my laptop and sip a small coffee with a piece of banana bread.</p><p>Starbucks&#8217; mission reads: &#8220;To be the premier purveyor of the finest coffee in the world, inspiring and nurturing the human spirit, one person, one cup and one neighborhood at a time&#8221;. In a European city, where coffee culture runs deep, differentiating on product quality is nearly impossible. But &#8220;inspiring and nurturing the human spirit&#8221; is not about the coffee. It is about how you feel when you walk in. And from that window the other day, I saw exactly that: people inspired by the environment.</p><p>Starbucks knows this so well that when they betrayed their own mission, the results collapsed. In 2024, new CEO Brian Niccol launched the &#8220;Back to Starbucks&#8221; plan, closing around 80-90 pickup-only stores and announcing 25,000 new seats across the US. The reason? Formats optimized for speed had eroded the experience. Every store, Niccol said, should be a place where people feel welcome and come together. When you optimize for convenience at the expense of your mission, you lose what makes you unique.</p><p>Now think about your institution. What happens when a visitor asks a question to one of your staff members? Colleen Dilenschneider, one of the most respected analysts in the American cultural sector, found that personal staff interactions are the single most reliable factor for increasing visitor satisfaction. Not a new wing. Not a spectacular temporary exhibition. The attention of a person. And the reverse is equally true: rude staff is the number one reason for visitor dissatisfaction, ahead of any access or infrastructure problem.</p><p>This means <strong>your mission cannot be a text hanging behind the ticket desk. It has to be the tool that every person on your team uses to make decisions, every day, in ordinary moments and in urgent ones.</strong></p><p>I am speaking especially to permanent cultural institutions, whose primary audience is their local community. Try answering these three questions:</p><ol><li><p>When a visitor leaves your space, what do you want them to tell a friend?</p></li><li><p>In a moment of operational crisis, which principle guides your staff&#8217;s decision?</p></li><li><p>If you removed your name from the building, would a visitor still recognize your identity from the experience alone?</p></li></ol><p>If you cannot answer clearly, your mission is not activated yet.</p><p>Everything you do, from visitor services to programming to events, must move in one direction. Your visitor may have never read your mission. But it is essential that they breathe it in every moment spent with you, when they think about you, and when they tell someone else about you.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Your board doesn’t say no to your idea. It says no to missing data.]]></title><description><![CDATA[The only method that turns "let's see" into "let's do it." And a free toolkit to do it yourself.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-board-doesnt-say-no-to-your</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-board-doesnt-say-no-to-your</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 11:01:59 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!94w0!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4303cf9d-328f-47fb-8bfb-5aa6e1a520c5_1344x768.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!94w0!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4303cf9d-328f-47fb-8bfb-5aa6e1a520c5_1344x768.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!94w0!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4303cf9d-328f-47fb-8bfb-5aa6e1a520c5_1344x768.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!94w0!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimag