craft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Many of you, replying to the welcome email of this newsletter, told me that one of your biggest struggles is getting your ideas approved by the people above you. New visit formats, digital tools, events for a different audience. Ideas you know are right, but that stop at a &#8220;let&#8217;s see&#8221;, which really means &#8220;no&#8221;.</p><p>I get it. And I&#8217;ll tell you right away the only method that works to turn that &#8220;let&#8217;s see&#8221; into a &#8220;let&#8217;s do it&#8221;.</p><p>It&#8217;s not well-crafted presentations. It&#8217;s not enthusiasm. It&#8217;s not even showing what other museums are doing. <strong>The only thing that convinces decision-makers is data. </strong>Your data, collected in your context, about your audience.</p><h2>A bookmark that cost zero</h2><p>A museum I worked with had a simple problem: visitors weren&#8217;t downloading the app. The team thought the problem was the app itself. Too complicated, too slow, not interesting enough.</p><p>Before redesigning anything, they tried an experiment that cost nothing. A paper bookmark, handed out with the ticket, with one sentence and a QR code to download the app.</p><p>Downloads quadrupled.</p><p>The problem wasn&#8217;t the app. It was that nobody knew it existed. A piece of paper produced a data point that weeks of meetings had failed to generate.</p><p>This is a Proof of Concept. Not a hundred-thousand-euro pilot project. <strong>A small, cheap, measurable experiment designed to produce a data point you didn&#8217;t have before.</strong></p><h2>Why your board needs proof</h2><p>It&#8217;s not just your director being cautious. It&#8217;s a systemic trait across the sector. A 2025 study surveying 300 leaders of cultural organisations found that 56% of institutions avoid change without overwhelming proof of its effectiveness. At the same time, 23% of leaders admit they are not using data effectively to make decisions.</p><p>The result is a paradox. <strong>The board demands proof to say yes, but nobody on the team is collecting that proof in a structured way.</strong> The Proof of Concept breaks this deadlock, because it&#8217;s so small and so cheap that it doesn&#8217;t need approval. <strong>It only needs someone willing to take responsibility for doing it.</strong></p><h2>The method in 4 steps</h2><p><strong>1. Reduce the idea to its testable core</strong></p><p>You don&#8217;t need to prove the idea will work at full scale. You need to prove the principle behind the idea is valid. If you want to add interactive stations for children along the exhibition path, don&#8217;t design ten of them. Start with one station, in one room, for four weekends.</p><p><strong>2. Decide what to measure before you start</strong></p><p>This is the step almost everyone skips. Metrics must be simple and collectible without infrastructure. For the children&#8217;s station: how long families stay in the room (compared to rooms without the station), how many families stop, a sheet of paper with a single question (&#8221;Would your child come back to this room?&#8221;). You don&#8217;t need sensors. You need a stopwatch and a notepad.</p><p><strong>3. Keep costs so low that nobody can say no</strong></p><p>The POC doesn&#8217;t need to produce the final result. It needs to produce the data that justifies the investment for the final result. The children&#8217;s station could be a table with drawing materials inspired by the artworks in the room. Cost: a few dozen euros. The real effort is in measuring, not building.</p><p><strong>4. Present the results, not the opinion</strong></p><p>The difference between &#8220;I believe families would appreciate children&#8217;s activities&#8221; and &#8220;over four weekends, families stayed an average of 23 minutes in the room with the station, compared to 7 in rooms without&#8221; is the difference between an ignored idea and an approved budget.</p><h2>It works at larger scale too</h2><p>If you think this approach only applies to small changes, there&#8217;s a case worth knowing. An American heritage site tested a new visitor circulation system with temporary, low-cost modifications. Annual attendance rose from 28,000 to 38,000, surpassing the previous record. But the number itself isn&#8217;t the point. The pilot generated the data needed to support future investment and gave leadership tangible proof of what a permanent change could achieve.</p><p>The principle is the same whether you run a museum with 10,000 visitors or 100,000. First the test, then the data, then the decision.</p><h2>The half-page document</h2><p>If tomorrow you want to present a POC to your director, the structure is this:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Problem observed.</strong> Families with children make up 35% of weekend visitors, but their average visit duration is 40 minutes compared to 65 minutes for couples without children.</p></li><li><p><strong>Hypothesis.</strong> A dedicated interactive station for children in Room X would increase family visit duration.</p></li><li><p><strong>Proposed test.</strong> A station with drawing materials, active for 4 weekends. Estimated cost: &#8364;80.</p></li><li><p><strong>What we will measure.</strong> Time spent in the room, number of interactions, family feedback.</p></li><li><p><strong>Decision point.</strong> If visit duration increases by 30%+ and feedback is positive from 70%+ of families, we propose extending to 3 rooms.</p></li></ul><p>No reasonable director says no to an &#8364;80 test that could improve the experience for a third of weekend visitors.</p><h2>The full toolkit</h2><p>I&#8217;ve put together a toolkit with everything you need to build your own Proof of Concept: the expanded framework with guiding questions for each step, the board document template ready to fill in, and a metrics checklist for five common types of experiments in the cultural sector.</p><p>You can download it for free here: <a href="https://www.claudiobuda.com/poc-toolkit/?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_medium=email&amp;utm_campaign=poc_toolkit">Get the POC Toolkit</a></p><h2>That face I hit on my own</h2><p>Over the years, I never had to convince a board. Since I was the one making decisions for my agency&#8217;s products, I hit my face on the wall alone. I made bad investments with nobody stopping me, and I wish someone had shown me this method earlier.</p><p>But precisely because I failed without a safety net, I know the lesson is always the same. <strong>Without data you&#8217;re just guessing. Whether you need to convince a director or just yourself, the process doesn&#8217;t change: reduce, measure, decide.</strong></p><p>If you have an idea you care about and don&#8217;t know how to get it approved, reply to this email. I&#8217;d like to hear what you&#8217;re working on.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hands]]></title><description><![CDATA[The most powerful experience your museum owns is hidden in the basement]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/hands</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/hands</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 11:02:10 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F