es and shelves holding pigments, brushes and restoration tools; at the center, a young woman conservator in a simple lab coat restores a monumental 16th-century wooden Piet&#224; sculpture on a padded support table using small brushes and cotton swabs" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!ogcp!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F93d4ebe8-20ff-4803-bdaf-74be6f36b8c8_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Every museum has, somewhere in its building, a person bent over a work of art with precision tools, thin brushes, solvents, and decades of knowledge in their hands. A restorer at work.</p><p>This person is doing something extraordinary: touching an object that no one else is allowed to touch. Making decisions that will influence how that work appears for the next fifty years. Using techniques passed down through generations, a combination of tactile experience and chemical knowledge that no manual can fully teach.</p><p>And almost always, they do it in a room closed to the public.</p><p>This is a strategic mistake. Not because restoration needs an audience to function, but because <strong>the audience needs restoration to live an experience that no information panel, no audio guide, no video will ever replicate.</strong></p><div><hr></div><p>Last week I attended a meeting of a regional cultural cluster inside a Fine Arts Academy. After the assembly, they split us into groups to visit the studios, discover study programs, and walk through the workshops.</p><p>In these small rooms we witnessed actual restorations in progress. Wooden works, including a 16th-century Piet&#224;, and paintings of Madonna and Child. The professor guiding us explained how the graduating students were applying restoration techniques on canvas, wood, and paper.</p><p>It was not a demonstration prepared for us. <strong>It was real work. And precisely for that reason, it was magnetic.</strong></p><p>I have been thinking about it for days. Not about the restoration itself, but about the potential of that experience if it were accessible to museum visitors. About the fact that millions of people every year walk past restored works without knowing what it actually means to care for a 16th-century painting. Without ever having seen someone do it.</p><div><hr></div><p>The idea is not new, and those who have put it into practice have seen remarkable results.</p><p>In 2001, the Pinacoteca di Brera in Milan faced a practical problem: there was no space for a separate restoration lab. The solution was to place a full-height glass laboratory, designed by Ettore Sottsass, directly along the visitor route. As Interni Magazine reported, &#8220;that transparent object, which immediately acquired the name aquarium, became a center of attraction: inside there is the restorer who knows how to handle works of extreme value, showing the public even the parts that are usually hidden&#8221;.</p><p>The Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam brought this principle to the most ambitious scale ever attempted. In 2019 it launched Operation Night Watch, the largest restoration project ever carried out on Rembrandt&#8217;s masterpiece, conducted entirely inside a specially designed glass chamber visible to visitors. The painting is seen by more than two million people each year, and the public restoration has become an integral part of the experience.</p><p>The Smithsonian American Art Museum in Washington built the Lunder Conservation Center with floor-to-ceiling glass walls, making every aspect of conservation work visible. A permanent approach to what most institutions keep behind closed doors.</p><p>In Italy, the MAO (Museum of Oriental Art) in Turin recently began restoring its collection of Japanese armor with a pop-up laboratory set up in the Mazzonis room, right at the beginning of the exhibition route. Three independent restorers work there, interacting with the public. Director Davide Quadrio described the experience as &#8220;transformative&#8221;, observing that when a young visitor sees &#8220;the dismembered suits of armor that are sewn, cleaned and handled,&#8221; what the restorers transmit is &#8220;not only knowledge but above all the love for what they do&#8221;.</p><p>Salvatore Amura, CEO of Valore Italia and of the Botticino school, one of Italy&#8217;s top restoration centers, put it clearly in an interview with Interni Magazine: &#8220;It&#8217;s a new system that is already in place. It&#8217;s about involving the public in a new way of visiting, an experience that also includes watching maintenance work&#8221;. For him, &#8220;restoration is an adventure of discovery, made of trial and error, made of scientific investigations and the revelation that the work makes of itself&#8221;.</p><p>This is not theory. It is a practice that the most forward-thinking museums are already adopting.</p><div><hr></div><p>Now, imagine applying this principle on a more accessible scale. You do not need a multi-million euro glass chamber.</p><p><strong>Imagine a gallery that dedicates one room in its route to showing a restorer recovering a piece. An ancient library that makes the conservation of manuscripts visible. A modern art space that offers a corner where an artist works in real time.</strong></p><p>If it is not possible to include this activity within the exhibition spaces, it could be an additional option in the itinerary. Something to do before or after the visit. Optional, but high value.</p><p>The point is that the visitor does not need to do anything special to live this experience. They just need to be there. And<strong> the magic comes from the authenticity of the moment</strong>: it is not a reconstruction, not a video, not a panel. It is a real person working on a real work, in real time.</p><p>I have imagined visitors interacting with the restorer, asking questions. And unless the restorer refuses contact with the public, they will almost always be happy to answer, explaining what they are doing. People tend to feel admiration and reverence for these meticulous figures. It is not a confrontational relationship. It is one of curiosity and respect.</p><div><hr></div><p>There is an advantage in all this that is often overlooked, and it does not only concern the visitor.</p><p>For restorers, contact with the public is an opportunity to understand what people consider important in a restoration. What questions they ask, what strikes them, what they ignore. Those people, tomorrow, could be private clients. Collectors who need work done on a family piece. Local administrators who must decide how to allocate funds for the conservation of a monument.</p><p>Visible restoration is not only a touchpoint for the visitor. It is a listening channel for the restorer. And in a profession where word of mouth and personal reputation matter more than any advertising campaign, that interaction has concrete value.</p><div><hr></div><p>There is something that happens when a visitor sees someone carefully touching a work that everyone else is forbidden to touch. It creates an emotional connection with the object that no other form of cultural mediation can produce.</p><p>Emanuela Daffra, Superintendent of the Opificio delle Pietre Dure in Florence, described it precisely: &#8220;For visitors it is very fascinating to see people putting their hands on the works because they are things that are usually forbidden to touch. And then suddenly visitors realize how important the material is in works of art that tend to become incorporeal or pure images&#8221;.</p><p>That is the shift. From the image to the material. From the incorporeal to the physical. From looking at a painting to understanding that it is made of layers, pigments, wood, canvas, and centuries of human decisions.</p><div><hr></div><p>If you run a museum, if you curate a collection, ask yourself: where are your restorers right now? What are they doing? And what would change in your exhibition if visitors could see them?</p><p><strong>The hands that care for the works are the show your museum does not know it has yet.</strong></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Who's it all really for?]]></title><description><![CDATA[On museum apps, simplicity, and what cultural institutions can really change.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/whos-it-all-really-for</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/whos-it-all-really-for</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 17 Jun 2026 11:03:33 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/b9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1260145,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A contemporary art museum scene: an overwhelmed female visitor with a bulky over-ear audio guide headset, juggling several smartphones and crumpled paper maps, stands before a large table interactive digital installation that overexplains the exhibition route while blocking her path.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/201570473?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A contemporary art museum scene: an overwhelmed female visitor with a bulky over-ear audio guide headset, juggling several smartphones and crumpled paper maps, stands before a large table interactive digital installation that overexplains the exhibition route while blocking her path." title="A contemporary art museum scene: an overwhelmed female visitor with a bulky over-ear audio guide headset, juggling several smartphones and crumpled paper maps, stands before a large table interactive digital installation that overexplains the exhibition route while blocking her path." srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BXLb!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb9204242-2548-44c2-b2d3-4d5b61537395_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>How often, over the past few months, have you added something new for your audience without stopping to ask whether <strong>it was making their life harder</strong>?</p><p>A dedicated app. Another QR code. A parallel digital path. An immersive experience to download before they even walk in. All of it well-intentioned, often well-executed. The problem is, the numbers tell a different story from the one we expect.</p><p>A study published in late 2025 by Nuseum, looking at 157 native museum apps in Germany and Austria, calculated an average pickup rate of 1.3%, with 90% of those apps used by fewer than 3% of visitors (Nuseum, &#8220;Museum App Statistics 2025&#8221;). A parallel analysis by Nubart on the audio guide segment reaches a strikingly similar conclusion: only 2.47% of visitors download the dedicated app. We&#8217;re investing tens of thousands of euros a year to reach 2-3% of the people who walk through our doors.</p><p>Let me be clear about one thing, because digital is what I do for a living: <strong>I&#8217;m not saying digital doesn&#8217;t work. It works beautifully, when it removes friction.</strong> The problem isn&#8217;t the technology. It&#8217;s the barrier to entry. A native app requires the visitor to install it, accept permissions, and spend precious time on setup before the visit has even started. And if you go the web-app route but 30% of the links are broken, you&#8217;re worse off than before. A poorly maintained tool is more damaging than a debatable tech choice.</p><p><strong>I&#8217;m writing to you today as someone who, until recently, was on the other side of the table.</strong></p><p>For years I thought I wanted to build a company that did something special. I&#8217;d wake up asking myself what I could invent next, rather than what I could solve for somebody else. It&#8217;s a typical distortion for people who are engineers first and makers second, and this year I had to look it in the face. It wasn&#8217;t easy.</p><p>The point is obvious, but for those of us who build, it really isn&#8217;t: nobody buys something to make the seller happy. <strong>They buy it because they need it to live better.</strong> It&#8217;s true for your visitors, it&#8217;s true for you when you&#8217;re choosing a tool, it&#8217;s true for everyone.</p><p>So I started doing something different. I began exploring what actually weighs on you every morning, and building from there. A concrete example. The payoff line of our product Showtime! currently reads &#8220;Activate your community&#8221;. The implication: you have a dormant audience you can&#8217;t reach, we&#8217;ll help. But talking to you, I&#8217;m discovering something else. You already know the potential of that community, very well. What you&#8217;re missing is a tool that lets you activate it as simply as possible, giving you back time for the strategic work you actually love, without being a slave to ten tools you have to coordinate by hand. That payoff line will probably change, because the real problem isn&#8217;t awareness. It&#8217;s friction.</p><p>And here I come back to the opening question, because it applies to you in relation to your audience too. How much are you actually simplifying their experience? Every time you introduce something new, do you stop to assess the overall impact, or do you just add and move on?</p><p>The &#8220;Curating Connection&#8221; report published in November 2025 by the agency Manifesto, cited by the Museums Association, says something interesting: 47% of visitors walk into a cultural institution driven by curiosity and a desire to learn. The demand is there, and it&#8217;s strong. What gets lost along the way is the connection, and it gets lost when the experience becomes more complex than they&#8217;re willing to tolerate in exchange for what you&#8217;re offering.</p><p><strong>There&#8217;s a widespread belief that to create a memorable experience, you have to accept making it more complex. Not true. It can absolutely be more complex for you to manage behind the scenes, but for the audience it has to be simple from second one.</strong></p><p>A few concrete examples of what I mean, because I don&#8217;t want to stay abstract. A clear welcome at the entrance that explains exactly how to access the guide, and someone available to help those who struggle. Different paths depending on the time the visitor has, because someone with thirty minutes and someone with three hours don&#8217;t want the same experience. Time slots designed for different audiences, instead of asking a school group and a contemporary art enthusiast to coexist at the same hour. A simple pre-visit message, maybe the day before, that says what to bring, where to park and how long it will last, to reduce the context anxiety every visitor carries in with them. One main action per touchpoint, because asking people to scan a QR, sign up for the newsletter and leave a review all at once is the best way to get none of the three. And then, mundanely, regular maintenance of digital installations, with the rule of removing what doesn&#8217;t work before the visitor discovers it for you. Nothing revolutionary. Just constant attention.</p><p>You know how much I care about less-is-more. <strong>I genuinely believe simplicity is the key, a bit like a recipe that works with few ingredients, carefully chosen.</strong></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The courage to rethink the path]]></title><description><![CDATA[What a castle bookshop taught me about timing, curation, and visitor behaviour]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-courage-to-rethink-the-path</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-courage-to-rethink-the-path</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 10 Jun 2026 11:02:37 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/e0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1153551,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A cozy museum gift shop interior in a medieval castle, stone walls and wooden beams, with neatly arranged suits of armor and medieval souvenirs on wooden racks, each shirt featuring a small, minimalist sailboat illustration on the chest, warm ambient lighting, detailed and inviting atmosphere&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/200422023?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A cozy museum gift shop interior in a medieval castle, stone walls and wooden beams, with neatly arranged suits of armor and medieval souvenirs on wooden racks, each shirt featuring a small, minimalist sailboat illustration on the chest, warm ambient lighting, detailed and inviting atmosphere" title="A cozy museum gift shop interior in a medieval castle, stone walls and wooden beams, with neatly arranged suits of armor and medieval souvenirs on wooden racks, each shirt featuring a small, minimalist sailboat illustration on the chest, warm ambient lighting, detailed and inviting atmosphere" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!H1cU!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe0ff3ef6-5625-4624-9d8f-5075129e47cb_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>The last castle I visited gave me an experience I wasn&#8217;t expecting.</p><p>Admission was free. Inside, a caf&#233; with a stunning view and signs for temporary events that weren&#8217;t running at the time. The visit was entirely outdoors, with plenty of stairs to climb, but absolutely worth it.</p><p>The woman at the entrance kindly asked where I was from, for statistical purposes, and then invited me to change my natural route to walk through a new, well-furnished room: a bookshop.</p><p><strong>Not a standard bookshop. No piles of random objects and trinkets on the shelves, just a few products, well displayed.</strong></p><p>The castle was in a city I was already exploring, so many of the items on sale were already connected to me before I even started the visit. Seeing them at the beginning felt surprisingly pleasant. As if the shop were a natural part of the itinerary. <strong>I actually wanted to browse, without the tiredness and the rush to leave that I so often feel in other museums.</strong></p><h2>Museum fatigue is real, and the bookshop at the end pays the price</h2><p>What I felt in that castle has a precise name in visitor behaviour research: <strong>museum fatigue</strong>. The term has existed since 1916, when Benjamin Ives Gilman published the first study on how museum visitors experience a progressive decline in attention and interest as their visit goes on. More than a century later, the research says the same thing. A study at the Florida Museum of Natural History observed a clear pattern: high interest for about 30 minutes, then a steady decline.</p><p>What does this have to do with the bookshop? Everything. If your shop is placed after an hour-long visit, you&#8217;re asking people to buy at the moment when they have the least energy, the least curiosity, and the most urgency to leave. It&#8217;s not a product problem. It&#8217;s a timing problem.</p><h2>Fewer choices, more purchases</h2><p>The other thing that struck me about that bookshop was the selection. Few products, well displayed, with space around them. No shelves packed to the brim.</p><p>There&#8217;s a classic experiment in consumer psychology, conducted by Sheena Iyengar at Columbia University, that explains why. In a supermarket, a display with 24 varieties of jam attracted 60% of the people passing by. But only 3% bought something. A display with 6 varieties? It attracted 40% of passers-by, and 30% bought. Ten times more.</p><p>The concept is called the &#8220;paradox of choice,&#8221; and it applies everywhere. Including museums. When a visitor walks into a bookshop crammed with hundreds of items, their brain goes into overload. Too many options, too little time, no guidance. And the most common reaction is: buy nothing.</p><p>A curated selection does the opposite. It reduces mental effort, increases satisfaction, and makes purchasing more likely. A study on perceived value in museum shops found that visitors prioritise aesthetic quality and representativeness: they prefer unique items connected to the place. In well-designed retail environments, willingness to spend can increase by up to 50%.</p><h2>The bookshop at the beginning: not a risk, but a priming</h2><p>Placing the bookshop before the visit sounds counterintuitive. The visitor hasn&#8217;t experienced anything yet. They&#8217;re not emotionally &#8220;charged&#8221;. Why would they buy?</p><p>In consumer psychology, there&#8217;s a concept called <strong>priming</strong>: exposure to an initial stimulus influences subsequent decisions, often entirely unconsciously. When you enter a supermarket and the first thing you see is fresh flowers and colourful fruit, your mood improves. And people in a better mood tend to spend more.</p><p>The same principle works in a museum. Seeing products at the beginning isn&#8217;t about making people buy right away. It&#8217;s about creating anticipation. The visitor sees a book about the city, a handcrafted object, a print. They don&#8217;t buy, but they register it. During the visit, that image works in the background. And when they walk back towards the exit (if the route allows it), the decision is already half made.</p><p>The American Alliance of Museums puts it clearly: positioning the shop so it&#8217;s visible at both entrance and exit is one of the most effective tactics for increasing sales. It builds immediate awareness of available products and then capitalises on the heightened interest generated by the visit.</p><h2>What that castle got right</h2><p>Looking back, that bookshop did at least four things well:</p><p><strong>Strategic positioning.</strong> The shop was before the visit, not after. The visitor arrives fresh, curious, with no rush. Time works in favour of the shopping experience, not against it.</p><p><strong>Curated selection.</strong> Few products, carefully chosen, with room to breathe. No cognitive overload, no pressure. Just items that made sense in that context.</p><p><strong>Connection to the place.</strong> The products weren&#8217;t generic. They were tied to the city I was visiting. That personal connection (I was already in a relationship with those themes) transformed the purchase from impulse into meaning.</p><p><strong>No pressure.</strong> Nobody tried to sell me anything. The invitation was simply &#8220;walk through this room&#8221;. The gentleness of that invitation made all the difference.</p><h2>The risk that isn&#8217;t a risk</h2><p>The legitimate concern is: if the visitor hasn&#8217;t experienced anything yet, they won&#8217;t feel ready to buy. And it&#8217;s true, they probably won&#8217;t buy on the first pass. But the absence of pressure and the time to process during the visit are equally valuable. The visitor observes, registers, thinks about it while climbing the stairs. And if the route brings them back to the starting point before leaving, they return with a clearer intention.</p><p><strong>It&#8217;s not about pushing people to buy. It&#8217;s about giving the visitor the time and space to get there on their own.</strong> And in the cultural sector, that&#8217;s not just a commercial strategy. <strong>It&#8217;s a sign of respect for the people who visit you.</strong></p><h2>How much is the bookshop worth to a cultural institution?</h2><p>For anyone managing a museum or cultural site, these aren&#8217;t just theoretical reflections. Well-managed museum shops can represent up to 25% of an institution&#8217;s total revenue. And in an era when public funding is steadily shrinking (in the US, for example, it dropped from 40% of museum income in 1989 to 24% in 2009, stabilising around that figure for the past 15 years), cultural retail has become a structural revenue source, not an accessory one.</p><p>Deshmane&#8217;s research (2024) on the relationship between visitor engagement and purchasing behaviour in museum shops found that museums offering greater interaction and immersion see a significant increase in both the quantity and value of sales. And McIntyre (2010) highlighted something even deeper: <strong>visitors perceive the shop as an integral part of the experience, not something separate.</strong> The separation, if it exists, lives only in the minds of those managing the museum.</p><h2>Having the courage to rethink the path</h2><p>That castle made a non-obvious choice. It took the bookshop, removed it from where everyone puts it, and moved it where nobody expects it. It reduced the selection instead of expanding it. It eliminated pressure instead of increasing it.</p><p>And it works. Not because it&#8217;s a genius move, but because it respects the way people actually behave. Not how we think they behave.</p><p>Sometimes the most courageous choice for a cultural institution isn&#8217;t adopting the latest digital tool, but rethinking something much simpler: the path a visitor takes from the moment they walk in to the moment they leave. And having the courage to turn it upside down.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[If a Technology Doesn’t Free Up Time for What Matters, You Don’t Need It]]></title><description><![CDATA[AI won&#8217;t fulfill your mission. More time for curating, welcoming, and storytelling will.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/if-a-technology-doesnt-free-up-time</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/if-a-technology-doesnt-free-up-time</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 03 Jun 2026 11:03:02 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/e85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1247287,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A cheerful Black male museum curator with a close-cropped afro, wearing a casual grey t-shirt and jeans, stands shoulder to shoulder with a compact, utilitarian humanoid robot with a matte black finish. They are in a cluttered, industrial-chic museum office space with exposed brick and metal shelving, during a rainy winter twilight. Both are smiling, reviewing blueprints on a large monitor, happily collaborating on museum work.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/199446684?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A cheerful Black male museum curator with a close-cropped afro, wearing a casual grey t-shirt and jeans, stands shoulder to shoulder with a compact, utilitarian humanoid robot with a matte black finish. They are in a cluttered, industrial-chic museum office space with exposed brick and metal shelving, during a rainy winter twilight. Both are smiling, reviewing blueprints on a large monitor, happily collaborating on museum work." title="A cheerful Black male museum curator with a close-cropped afro, wearing a casual grey t-shirt and jeans, stands shoulder to shoulder with a compact, utilitarian humanoid robot with a matte black finish. They are in a cluttered, industrial-chic museum office space with exposed brick and metal shelving, during a rainy winter twilight. Both are smiling, reviewing blueprints on a large monitor, happily collaborating on museum work." srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!htQf!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe85e74ac-0c48-4a7b-ba65-61c34bfc0d11_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p></p><p>I think about how often a cultural team adopts a new tool and six months later has more work than before, not less. A CMS to populate, a management system to feed, a social channel to maintain. The promise was &#8220;it will simplify your life&#8221;. The reality is one more tab open in the browser and one more procedure to follow.</p><p>With AI, the same thing is happening. Only faster.</p><h2>The focus we&#8217;re losing</h2><p>Last month I facilitated an AI workshop with a group of professionals. I didn&#8217;t talk about prompts, language models, or which tool is better than the others. <strong>I put one question at the center: what problems do you already have today that nobody is solving?</strong></p><p>The discussion was lively, but that question forced everyone to think differently. Because the truth is that the rush toward &#8220;being more productive with AI&#8221; is making us <strong>lose sight of a more important question: productive to do what?</strong></p><p>For a software company like mine, the answer is simple: solve problems for clients and make money. For a cultural organization, the mission is broader and more delicate. A museum exists to preserve, to tell stories, to welcome. A theatre exists to create live experiences that stay with you. A festival exists to build moments of belonging.</p><p>None of these missions are fulfilled by &#8220;being more productive.&#8221; They are fulfilled by having more time for the things that matter.</p><h2>Think of AI as new team members, not as software</h2><p>Reflecting on that workshop, there&#8217;s a shift in perspective I want to propose.</p><p>When you bring a new person into your team, you don&#8217;t put them to work independently right away. First you help them understand how the group works, what the dynamics are, who does what. Then you teach them the processes. Integration first, training second.</p><p>AI should work the same way. Think of it as a handful of competent, fast people you need to bring into your organization. Not as software to install. This lets you think more clearly about where they can actually help and where they would only create confusion.</p><p>And like any new team member, there will be some distrust. That&#8217;s natural. A curator who has spent years developing their own narrative voice won&#8217;t welcome with open arms a tool that &#8220;writes for them.&#8221; And they&#8217;re right to want control over that process, because a museum&#8217;s voice is built on years of curatorial judgment. It&#8217;s not something you hand off entirely and hope for the best.</p><p>But wanting control doesn&#8217;t mean doing everything yourself. AI shouldn&#8217;t sideline the curator. It should work under their direction, handling the heavy lifting so they can focus on the decisions that actually shape the visitor&#8217;s experience.</p><h2>Where it can actually help you</h2><p>Think about the curator who spends three hours translating exhibition texts for a temporary show into three languages. That time could go toward meeting the artists, studying the context, improving the exhibition layout. Or think about the marketing manager at a theatre who every Monday morning manually compiles the same report on last week&#8217;s ticket sales. Those two hours could become two hours of real analysis on what&#8217;s working and what&#8217;s not in how they communicate with their audience.</p><p>I&#8217;m not telling you to &#8220;use AI for everything&#8221;. I&#8217;m telling you: <strong>look at your daily work and ask yourself where time gets absorbed by repetitive tasks that don&#8217;t require your professional judgment</strong>. Translations to review, data to organize, information to search for, routine communications to prepare.</p><p>If you don&#8217;t have someone in-house with the skills to map where AI can make a difference, find that person externally. Explain your process pain points and work together to figure out where AI can fit in without disrupting what already works.</p><h2>The real measure</h2><p>The time a technology frees up has value only if you know where to reinvest it. For those who work in culture, the important things are those no technology will ever do in your place:</p><ul><li><p>Standing in front of visitors and understanding what they feel. </p></li><li><p>Solving the problems that only surface when you&#8217;re present. </p></li><li><p>Welcoming people in a way that makes them feel they belong. </p></li><li><p>Studying, staying current, cultivating the expertise that makes your work unique.</p></li></ul><p>If your new AI tool isn&#8217;t giving you more time for these things, think again. Maybe it&#8217;s not the right tool, maybe you&#8217;re not using it the right way, or maybe you didn&#8217;t need it at all.</p><p>The best technology is the one that makes you forget you have it. Because it already gave you back the time to do what truly matters.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[A Paper Card Just Outperformed Every Feedback Tablet]]></title><description><![CDATA[Why a branded paper card collected more honest feedback than any screen ever could.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/a-paper-card-just-outperformed-every</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/a-paper-card-just-outperformed-every</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 27 May 2026 11:03:32 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1280645,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A person elegantly exiting a grand theater entrance and carefully inserting a voting card into one of two ballot boxes with a finely crafted wooden base&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/198675975?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A person elegantly exiting a grand theater entrance and carefully inserting a voting card into one of two ballot boxes with a finely crafted wooden base" title="A person elegantly exiting a grand theater entrance and carefully inserting a voting card into one of two ballot boxes with a finely crafted wooden base" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WVD9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F402a3cef-12ce-42ef-8f4a-fedf0e8ca19e_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>At the theatre entrance, while my ticket is being checked, a hand gives me a small paper card. Branded, colourful, the size of a business card. I don&#8217;t immediately understand what it&#8217;s for.</p><p>Then they explain: at the end of the show, I can cast my vote by dropping it into one of the urns at the exit. One for positive, one for negative.</p><p>I spent the entire performance fidgeting with that card between my fingers. And when I left, I dropped it into the negative urn, because I didn&#8217;t enjoy the show. But here&#8217;s the point: despite my negative experience, I participated in the feedback process with genuine enthusiasm. And there&#8217;s an important lesson in this for anyone running a cultural institution.</p><h2>The best feedback is the one that doesn&#8217;t feel like feedback</h2><p>Think about it. <strong>The biggest problem with collecting feedback isn&#8217;t the tool, it&#8217;s participation. </strong>Only the enthusiasts and the angry ones respond. Everyone else, the silent majority, just walks out.</p><p>That little card solves exactly this problem, because it turns a task into a game. They&#8217;re not asking you to fill out a form. They&#8217;re giving you a physical object to interact with during the experience. By the time you reach the urn, the gesture feels natural. It requires no effort, no time, and it doesn&#8217;t interrupt the flow of leaving.</p><p>I&#8217;ve written before about <a href="https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/museum-feedback-faces-whats-the-point">the smiley-face tablets</a> you find at the exit of many museums. Those devices work, but they have a structural limitation: the visitor has to stop, look at a screen, and consciously decide to give feedback. With the card, that decision was already made at the entrance. The visitor just has to complete a gesture.</p><h2>Three things this system does better than a tablet</h2><p><strong>Zero friction.</strong> No app to download, no wifi needed, no interface to figure out. It works for anyone, at any age, in any language.</p><p><strong>Residual branding.</strong> In the worst case scenario, the visitor who doesn&#8217;t vote takes the card home. A branded object that stays in a pocket, on a nightstand, in a drawer. Every time they see it again, the institution comes back to mind. Try getting the same result from a tablet.</p><p><strong>Instant flexibility.</strong> Want to change the question? Change the label on the urn. &#8220;Did you enjoy the show?&#8221; today, &#8220;Would you come back for another title in this season?&#8221; tomorrow. On a tablet, this kind of change might require technical support or access to a platform that isn&#8217;t always under your control.</p><h2>Yes, but how much does it cost?</h2><p>Less than you think. A thousand printed cards from a local printer cost a few euros and cover several evenings. The production fits right into the promotional materials you&#8217;re already printing every season, so the budget impact is marginal.</p><p>Counting takes a few minutes at the end of each evening. Anyone can do it, even a volunteer during downtime between shows. If you want more precision, a mechanical hand counter costs less than ten euros and gives you an exact number without physically counting each card.</p><p><strong>The real cost isn&#8217;t financial, it&#8217;s creative.</strong> Someone has to think up this system, design the card, choose the right questions, analyse the results over time. But that&#8217;s true for any feedback system, digital or not.</p><h2>The strategic takeaway</h2><p>I&#8217;m not saying analogue is always better than digital. That would be absurd, especially coming from me. But in an era where the default answer to any problem is &#8220;let&#8217;s put a tablet there&#8221; or &#8220;let&#8217;s build an app&#8221;, well-designed analogue solutions have an unexpected competitive advantage: they&#8217;re memorable.</p><p>A tablet at the exit of a museum is invisible. We&#8217;ve seen it a hundred times. A card in your hand for the entire duration of a show is an experience.</p><p>For anyone running theatres, museums, or festivals, the right question isn&#8217;t &#8220;digital or analogue?&#8221; It&#8217;s &#8220;<strong>which solution generates more participation with less friction?</strong>&#8221; Sometimes the answer is software. Sometimes it&#8217;s a well-designed piece of paper.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Between one exhibition and the next, where does your time go?]]></title><description><![CDATA[A daily filter for deciding where your time actually belongs]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/between-one-exhibition-and-the-next</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/between-one-exhibition-and-the-next</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 20 May 2026 11:02:54 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1328877,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A temporary exhibition space arranged in a chaotic way, with open cardboard boxes, scattered packaging materials and partially disassembled display structures, a woman in motion running in the foreground with her hands in her hair and a surprised and tired expression, wide-angle shot&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/197482098?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A temporary exhibition space arranged in a chaotic way, with open cardboard boxes, scattered packaging materials and partially disassembled display structures, a woman in motion running in the foreground with her hands in her hair and a surprised and tired expression, wide-angle shot" title="A temporary exhibition space arranged in a chaotic way, with open cardboard boxes, scattered packaging materials and partially disassembled display structures, a woman in motion running in the foreground with her hands in her hair and a surprised and tired expression, wide-angle shot" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eJ2Q!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2fb54170-e8ef-48ba-a7af-aea75abc18f8_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Five days. That&#8217;s often all the time between tearing down a temporary exhibition and setting up the next one. In between: updating the website, preparing communications, answering dozens of emails, coordinating with suppliers, managing the gift shop, fixing the signage. And somewhere on that list, finding the time to actually think about what you&#8217;re doing.</p><p><strong>When I asked you what the most frustrating problem you face every week is, the most common answer was clear: not having enough time to do the job well. Not to do it. To do it </strong><em><strong>well</strong></em><strong>.</strong></p><p>I understand this frustration because I&#8217;ve seen it from the other side.</p><p>When we started selling apps to help you communicate with your audience, I was convinced our real strength was the result: a new channel, more visitors, more visibility. Over time I realized I was wrong. Our real strength was the support, the care we put into helping clients use our tools and solving their problems. Because if you&#8217;re working badly, if you&#8217;re overwhelmed, if you don&#8217;t have time to think, no result holds up. I&#8217;ve seen brilliant professionals leave cultural institutions not because they didn&#8217;t love the work, but because the work had stopped giving them room to express themselves. And when you lose those people, you lose everything.</p><p>So results matter, of course. But they matter as much as a working life that allows you to give your institution what it needs with clarity, not with panic.</p><p>There&#8217;s a framework I try to follow every day, and today I want to share it with you. It&#8217;s called <strong>EAD: Eliminate, Automate, Delegate</strong>. I didn&#8217;t invent it, but I&#8217;ve adapted it to our world and I use it as a daily filter to decide where to put my time.</p><h3>1. Eliminate</h3><p>This is the hardest part, because in the cultural sector everything feels important. But try looking at your typical week and asking yourself: what am I doing that doesn&#8217;t actually produce real value?</p><p>That weekly press roundup you prepare but nobody on the board actually reads. That monthly attendance report you copy by hand from three different sources that ends up in a drawer. That social media channel you keep open out of inertia with two posts a month and zero interactions.</p><p>Eliminating isn&#8217;t losing opportunities. It&#8217;s making space. Every activity you remove from the list gives you back time for the decisions that matter, the curatorial ones, the strategic ones, the ones only you can make.</p><h3>2. Automate</h3><p>Everything you can&#8217;t eliminate but that doesn&#8217;t require your unique skills can probably be automated.</p><p>Sending booking confirmations for school groups. Updating opening hours on the website when they change seasonally. Collecting post-visit feedback. Reminding colleagues about programming deadlines.</p><p>Software, and today at an accelerated pace artificial intelligence, can put into an automatic flow tasks that used to require hours of manual work. You don&#8217;t need advanced technical skills. You need to make a list of the repetitive things you do on your computer and ask your IT person to find a solution.</p><h3>3. Delegate</h3><p>Everything that&#8217;s left and that you can&#8217;t automate, try to delegate it. To junior colleagues, to suppliers, to external collaborators.</p><p>I know many of you struggle with this step. I&#8217;ve seen museum directors rewrite the text of a display panel after assigning it to a junior curator, because &#8220;it wasn&#8217;t exactly how I would have written it&#8221;. But delegating also means accepting that the result will be different from yours. Not worse, different. Let your colleagues get their hands dirty. Let them make mistakes. That&#8217;s the only way they&#8217;ll grow, and the only way you&#8217;ll understand whether they&#8217;re in the right role or whether it makes sense to look for someone else.</p><div><hr></div><p>Next time you find yourself in that chaos between one exhibition and the next, try doing one thing: pick one activity from your weekly list and run it through the EAD filter. Can I eliminate it? Can I automate it? Can I delegate it?</p><p>Just one. And if you feel like it, write to me and tell me which one you chose.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The audience you're losing for a decade]]></title><description><![CDATA[Most temporary exhibitions don't consider families. That's not a design choice. It's a missed opportunity.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-audience-youre-losing-for-a-decade</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-audience-youre-losing-for-a-decade</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 13 May 2026 11:02:15 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg" width="1456" height="1456" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1456,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1167379,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;a father and a young child seen from behind, standing side by side in a spacious contemporary art museum gallery, both quietly observing a large abstract painting on the wall, each wearing a single white AirPod in one ear&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/196754129?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="a father and a young child seen from behind, standing side by side in a spacious contemporary art museum gallery, both quietly observing a large abstract painting on the wall, each wearing a single white AirPod in one ear" title="a father and a young child seen from behind, standing side by side in a spacious contemporary art museum gallery, both quietly observing a large abstract painting on the wall, each wearing a single white AirPod in one ear" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!saX9!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F931a6087-af98-4395-89f5-7d4298c3f846_1920x1920.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Last week I visited a temporary exhibition by one of the most well-known contemporary artists of our time. The space was beautiful: curated rooms, an internal garden, a large and well-stocked bookshop at the end. The artist is known for his colourful, modern techniques applied to portraits, and I thought it would be a perfect visit to bring the kids along.</p><p>For them, it was the first real experience in a museum not specifically designed for children. We&#8217;ve already been to several science museums across Italy, the kind that are built with kids in mind. This time I wanted to try something different.</p><p>It was May 1st, and the place was packed. Maybe too packed, but I understand that limiting access on International Workers&#8217; Day would have been counterproductive. The exhibition was well curated, with an audioguide included in the ticket and just the right amount of information on the panels.</p><p>The problem was something else.</p><p><strong>No one had considered the possibility that a parent might visit that exhibition with children.</strong> No path designed for them. No touchpoint at their height. Nothing.</p><div class="subscription-widget-wrap-editor" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;}" data-component-name="SubscribeWidgetToDOM"><div class="subscription-widget show-subscribe"><div class="preamble"><p class="cta-caption">Thanks for reading The Cultural Journey Gourmet! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.</p></div><form class="subscription-widget-subscribe"><input type="email" class="email-input" name="email" placeholder="Type your email&#8230;" tabindex="-1"><input type="submit" class="button primary" value="Subscribe"><div class="fake-input-wrapper"><div class="fake-input"></div><div class="fake-button"></div></div></form></div></div><p>I get it. Four months of temporary exhibition. A dedicated path for kids might not seem like a priority. But with works that colourful, with techniques that visual and immediate, coming up with something for them wouldn&#8217;t have been hard.</p><p>So my partner and I did what parents do: we figured it out ourselves.</p><p>I pulled out my AirPods (I carry them everywhere now) and we took turns putting them on the kids, connected to the in-app audioguide. Each track became a &#8220;story.&#8221; For each one, I added my own translation into kid-friendly language, filled with little tales about the people in the portraits.</p><p>Maybe it was the novelty. Maybe it was the enthusiasm we passed on to them as we stood in front of those works. Maybe it was the right length, about an hour. In the end, the kids had a great time.</p><p>But if I had to do it again tomorrow, in the same format, I&#8217;d be a little worried. The novelty effect doesn&#8217;t work twice.</p><p>I&#8217;m not naming the space or the artist, because that&#8217;s not the point. The point is that this situation isn&#8217;t an isolated case. It&#8217;s the norm.</p><p><strong>We live in a time where parents need to have experiences with their children, who can&#8217;t always be left at home. If you don&#8217;t think about them, you risk cutting out adults for roughly a decade of their lives.</strong></p><p>Think about it from an organiser&#8217;s perspective. A parent with young kids is between 30 and 45 years old. They have spending power, they share experiences on social media, and if they enjoy themselves, they come back. You&#8217;re not losing one visitor. You&#8217;re losing two, three, four. And you&#8217;re losing them for years.</p><p>Simple things would have been enough. A table with a couple of colours. Audio stories designed for them too. A couple of installations placed at one metre high. All of this without ruining the experience for those visiting without children, which obviously remains the priority. But at the ticket price they were charging, the organisers could have thought about it. And framed this attention as a strength, not a compromise.</p><p>Next time you&#8217;re designing a temporary exhibition, ask yourselves one more question: <strong>what would a parent with a five-year-old do in here?</strong> If the answer is &#8220;I wouldn&#8217;t know,&#8221; there&#8217;s an opportunity you&#8217;re leaving on the table.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Where am I? ]]></title><description><![CDATA[When finding your way takes longer than enjoying the experience, something is broken.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/where-am-i</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/where-am-i</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 06 May 2026 11:01:26 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1351757,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/195845333?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!_jtI!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F544697c4-7903-4a8e-8f2c-73a693b91248_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p><strong>If your visitors spend more time searching for information than actually enjoying the experience, frustration goes through the roof.</strong> It doesn&#8217;t matter if you&#8217;re using an app, a website, or a paper map. The problem isn&#8217;t the channel. The problem is how much care you put into the user experience on that channel.</p><p>Last week I was at a large city-wide festival, full of installations and micro-experiences scattered across a historic city centre. At some point I walked up to a kiosk and, exhausted from opening and closing my phone, asked: &#8220;Can I get a paper map of the event?&#8221;</p><p>For someone who has spent years building digital tools to improve experiences, this is the question I never want to hear from a visitor. Abandoning the device for paper felt like a stab in the back.</p><p>I had already spent two hours bouncing between Google Maps and the festival website. I&#8217;d open Google Maps to figure out where I was. Then I&#8217;d switch to the event site, tap on points of interest that seemed close by. Then back to Google to double-check the street name. Then back to the site, which had reloaded and closed the map, forcing me to start over just to read the event details. A constant ping-pong between two apps that don&#8217;t talk to each other. At that point I actually missed the year they had released a native app, even though it was genuinely clunky.</p><p>But guess what? I only used the paper map once. The impossibility of locating myself in the city&#8217;s narrow streets and the time it took to decode dozens of numbered dots through the legend was a monumental effort. Once again, serious user experience problems.</p><p>And that&#8217;s when I asked myself a question: in all these years of running this festival, have the organizers ever sent someone out into the city to find the events and test for wayfinding issues?</p><p><strong>Every type of cultural experience has its most suitable support tools. But it&#8217;s the care for UX and UI that makes any tool effective. </strong>Even a paper map with a curated selection of points of interest marked directly on the map, instead of constantly sending you back to a dense legend, is something useful.</p><p>Think about itineraries, about geographic coordinates, about how important it is for your visitors to know where they are. I consider myself someone with a strong sense of direction. But if wayfinding is not part of the cultural experience and I want to maximise the time I spend on cultural content, making orientation simple is essential.</p><p>On printed panels, add &#8220;you are here&#8221; dots. On paper maps, stop sending users back to the legend. On web apps, geolocate the user. On native apps, ensure speed even without connectivity. These are small examples of how to perform well on whichever channel you choose.</p><p>Finally, do you have that one person in your office who gets lost even at the airport? Good. Ask them to try out your next temporary exhibition and, why not, evaluate how well your staff helps them find their way around.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The museum I would never have visited]]></title><description><![CDATA[Why a free museum about a sailing race almost made me buy a t-shirt]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-museum-i-would-never-have-visited</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-museum-i-would-never-have-visited</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 29 Apr 2026 11:03:16 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/bab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1299670,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A dynamic sailing regatta on a bright sunny day, choppy turquoise waves and numerous colorful sailboats racing in close competition, one boat in the foreground violently ramming another and sending dramatic sprays of water into the air, sails stretched tight by the strong wind, crew members bustling on deck adjusting ropes and leaning out over the water, crisp clear sky with a few soft white clouds, high-detail, cinematic composition, vibrant colors, sharp focus, sense of speed and intensity&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/195011674?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A dynamic sailing regatta on a bright sunny day, choppy turquoise waves and numerous colorful sailboats racing in close competition, one boat in the foreground violently ramming another and sending dramatic sprays of water into the air, sails stretched tight by the strong wind, crew members bustling on deck adjusting ropes and leaning out over the water, crisp clear sky with a few soft white clouds, high-detail, cinematic composition, vibrant colors, sharp focus, sense of speed and intensity" title="A dynamic sailing regatta on a bright sunny day, choppy turquoise waves and numerous colorful sailboats racing in close competition, one boat in the foreground violently ramming another and sending dramatic sprays of water into the air, sails stretched tight by the strong wind, crew members bustling on deck adjusting ropes and leaning out over the water, crisp clear sky with a few soft white clouds, high-detail, cinematic composition, vibrant colors, sharp focus, sense of speed and intensity" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!w88j!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbab6ab91-3a11-4ba0-9e54-00c7e46d44f2_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>A few weeks ago I was in Alicante with friends and we ended up at The Ocean Race Museum, dedicated to the round-the-world ocean race. My first reaction was: &#8220;A museum about a sailing race? Really?&#8221;.</p><p>I walked in skeptical. I almost walked out with a t-shirt.</p><p>And somewhere in between, I understood something that applies to any museum. Including yours.</p><h2>Stories, not labels</h2><p>That museum wasn&#8217;t describing boats. <strong>It was telling lives.</strong></p><p>There was the story of an edition where two boats rammed into each other during the race. There was the more tragic one, about a skipper who lost his life at sea, and his crew withdrawing from the competition. There were crew reportages, each with a different angle. The onboard doctor. The person in charge of supplies. The one handling maintenance. All of them telling what it meant to spend months on the open ocean.</p><p>And then a story that struck me in particular: a team that over the years had perfected their boat through nearly invisible adjustments, eventually becoming the undisputed champion of the race. They weren&#8217;t explaining the technology. They were telling you about the obsession.</p><p>Everywhere you could feel effort, success, joy and pain. Photos and testimonies. A blend of life and technique.</p><h2>The principle that applies to everyone</h2><p>Now think about your museum. <strong>How many of your panels, audio guides, and touchpoints describe what the visitor already sees?</strong></p><p>&#8220;Amphora, 3rd century BC, red-figure pottery, height 45 cm.&#8221;</p><p>The visitor is already looking at the amphora. They know it&#8217;s an amphora. What they don&#8217;t know is who used it, what it contained, where it was found and by whom. They don&#8217;t know that an archaeologist unearthed it on a rainy day after three months of fruitless digging, or that an identical amphora was found in a shipwreck two thousand kilometres away, which tells us something surprising about ancient trade routes.</p><p><strong>Those are stories. And stories do what labels cannot: they create a connection.</strong></p><p>Don&#8217;t waste the few touchpoints your visitor is willing to read or listen to by repeating what their eyes already see. Use those moments to tell them something that sparks curiosity and pushes them to go deeper. The technical details? Put them one level down, for those who want to know more. But the entry point into the experience must be a story.</p><p>Sometimes this is easy, especially if you run an anthropological museum or a site with well-documented history. Other times it&#8217;s harder, because you have artefacts of uncertain origin. But behind each of these objects there can be stories. Or you can build them, with proper research and context to back them up.</p><h2>The t-shirt</h2><p>I walked out of that museum thinking that the relationship between humans and the sea is ancient, and deeply moving. Groups of people joining forces to complete impossible missions, for survival or for sporting glory.</p><p>At the gift shop there was a white t-shirt with a simple logo. I was in a hurry and didn&#8217;t buy it. But I wanted to. <strong>I wanted to take home a piece of those stories, feel part of that life, those challenges, that strength. And share them with others.</strong></p><p>Think about it: a free museum about a sailing race had created in me the desire to spend money at the gift shop. Not because of the t-shirt design, but because of what it represented. Because they had told me stories.</p><p>If your visitor walks out wanting to take home a piece of your story, you&#8217;ve won. But that only happens if you&#8217;ve told them a story, not if you&#8217;ve described a collection.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The archaeology of QR codes]]></title><description><![CDATA[I spent more time deciphering panels than looking at the artefacts. That's when I understood what an audio guide is really for.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-archaeology-of-qr-codes</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-archaeology-of-qr-codes</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 22 Apr 2026 11:01:53 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/cad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1200612,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/194279563?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!8vbi!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcad437be-6a46-43f5-93eb-1ade18f73f06_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p><strong>An audio guide is not content. It is a synchronisation tool: it lets you contextualise what you are looking at in the exact moment you are looking at it.</strong> Without one, you end up reading panels instead of observing objects. And when the panels are in a language you don&#8217;t speak, you are left with nothing.</p><p>I was reminded of this last week, visiting an archaeological museum in a European city. The exhibition traced a path from prehistory to the present day, following the history of the region. It was well done, and one choice impressed me immediately: in every room, an ambient soundscape tied to the era. Grazing animals for prehistory, cart wheels, people at a harbour. A sensory layer that genuinely enhanced the visit.</p><p>But when I asked whether an audio guide was available, the answer was: &#8220;there are panels, they&#8217;re easy to read.&#8221; It was the second time in two months I had heard this. In this case, the panels were only in two local languages we didn&#8217;t know. English was virtually absent.</p><p>Then we were told that QR codes in the rooms link to audio tracks. Good, I thought. Let&#8217;s scan.</p><p>The QR code led to a web page with a tiny SoundCloud widget. Five tracks, no Italian, at least English was there. The buttons were so small that selecting the right track with an adult finger was nearly impossible. When I brought the phone to my ear to listen, I inevitably touched the screen and interrupted playback. When a track ended, it didn&#8217;t continue to the next stop but jumped to the same track in another language. After five attempts, I gave up.</p><p>This is not an isolated case. My experience fits a documented pattern: an academic study analysing digital interface usability across three museums identified 176 distinct problems, including the inability for users to understand where they were in the tour, confusing button layouts, and a disconnect between the digital interface and the physical museum space. <strong>The barrier here is not the visitor.</strong> QR code familiarity has surged from 35% pre-pandemic to 98% today. <strong>The problem is the implementation.</strong></p><p>I went back to the panels, trying to interpret the two local languages. <strong>And I realised I was spending more time deciphering text than looking at the artefacts.</strong> Halfway through the visit I was already exhausted, not because of the museum, but because of the effort required to compensate for a tool that did not work.</p><p>Here is the point. This is not a technical problem. It is a promise problem. That museum invested in the quality of its exhibition design, in its ambient soundscapes, in the care of its interiors. Then it delegated the audio guide to a free widget that no one ever tested with a phone in hand. <strong>The implicit message to the visitor is clear: we care about the experience up to a point.</strong></p><p>And here is a number that should concern anyone managing a museum. Research by Dimensional Research found that 95% of people who have a negative experience tell someone about it, compared to 87% of those who have a positive one. So far the gap seems small. But negative experiences are shared on social media 50% more often than positive ones, and more than half of those who share a bad experience do so more than five times. Considering that 37% of museum visits originate from word-of-mouth and recommendations (Manifesto/Censuswide, 2025), every frustrated visitor is not just a lost visit. It is a reputational damage multiplier.</p><p>If you are a cultural operator, there is a simple principle to take away: test every digital tool the way a visitor would use it, not the way the person who designed it would. Three seconds of friction repeated at every display case become thirty minutes of frustration. And a frustrated visitor does not return, does not recommend, does not leave a positive review.</p><p>An audio guide does not have to be perfect. It has to be usable. If it is not, it is better not to offer one at all. Because <strong>the absence of a service is neutral. A service that does not work is damage.</strong></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Who are you talking to?]]></title><description><![CDATA[84% of museums collect visitor data. Only 16% actually organize it. Before you communicate, you need to know who's listening.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/who-are-you-talking-to</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/who-are-you-talking-to</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 15 Apr 2026 11:03:21 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/a7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1167435,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/193556711?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gZLJ!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa7c5db57-f9fb-400a-9a25-e867b3ac592f_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Last week, a client told me about a contact in their database with 10 phone numbers. They couldn&#8217;t tell if it was one person with many numbers or 10 different people who happen to share the same name.</p><p>This is an extreme example of not knowing your audience. But before judging, a question: how well do you know yours?</p><p><strong>84% of Italian museums collect visitor data, but only 16% organize it in a structured system.</strong> Think about that. It&#8217;s like having a warehouse full of unlabeled boxes. You know there&#8217;s something useful inside, but you can never find it when you need it.</p><p>Do you have a clear picture of your target audience? It doesn&#8217;t have to be just one. Even if you&#8217;re not opening your doors today, you already have a community that has come into contact with you at some point. How well do you understand these people? Do you know them? Do you have a sense of how they move and what makes them feel something?</p><p>This isn&#8217;t about stalking Claudio to find out what he does on Saturday mornings. It&#8217;s about defining one or more user personas that represent the audience you want to reach, so you can explain in the right words what you can make them feel.</p><h2>Why communicate, and to whom</h2><p><strong>You offer a service that generates emotions. But if your target audience doesn&#8217;t know you exist, they&#8217;ll never choose you to experience those emotions</strong>. In fact, they&#8217;ll probably never experience them at all, given how unique each of your cultural organizations truly is.</p><p>Here&#8217;s a number that struck me: more than 80% of first-time visitors to a cultural institution never come back. Eighty out of a hundred. Not because the experience was bad, but because nobody built a bridge between that first visit and the second one. And that bridge starts with having a contact database that allows you to reach them again.</p><p>What&#8217;s your reason for communicating? I know something about this topic, because I&#8217;ve made every mistake there is to make.</p><h2>My 4-year mistake</h2><p>12 years ago I started a video blog. I wanted a bigger audience, and I never got one. The problem wasn&#8217;t the format, and it wasn&#8217;t consistency.</p><p>Every Friday evening, waiting to go out with friends, I&#8217;d spend a couple of hours on YouTube searching for new music with great videos that could inspire me. Beyond rediscovering stunning videos of rock songs I already loved, I found wonderful pieces of electronic and soul music that I still carry with me. Each post was a music video paired with a short personal story written in English about how I was feeling at the time, often tied to my love life. I was genuinely consistent, publishing almost every week for 4 years.</p><p>The problem was a mess of wrong target and wrong channel. Who was I talking to? Friends? Past relationships? The general public? People in Italy or people abroad? Not wanting to spend a cent on ads, I used my personal Facebook as a megaphone, supported by a dedicated page. The combination of a vague target and a channel where that target may or may not have been led to four years with zero followers.</p><p>One positive note? I eventually realized that channel ended up serving me. I still use it today as a music playlist whenever I want to listen to something I know I&#8217;ll enjoy.</p><p>In my case, I was communicating for a mix of reasons: to be seen as someone with good musical taste, to share what was troubling me deep down, to be perceived as a person with depth. Four years of consistent content, zero results. Because the target was never clear.</p><h2>Your contact database is an asset</h2><p>That contact with 10 phone numbers is not an isolated case. <strong>Messy, incomplete databases are the norm in the cultural sector. And in my view, this is the very first thing to fix the moment you decide to communicate.</strong></p><p>Contact data decays by 22-30% every year. People change jobs, emails, phone numbers. Without regular maintenance, your list loses a quarter of its value every twelve months. And every email that lands in the void is a missed opportunity to build a relationship.</p><p>But when an organization decides to put things in order, results follow. The New Wolsey Theatre, a 400-seat venue in Ipswich, UK, had its audience data scattered across box office, marketing, development, and administration, each with their own separate lists. When they unified everything into a single system and started segmenting communications based on attendance frequency, within twelve months they saw a 69% increase in audience members returning for eight or more shows and a 31% growth in box office revenue. Without spending more on acquisition. Simply by talking better to people who already knew them.</p><p>Clean it, refine it, delete what&#8217;s no longer useful or what you don&#8217;t have the right to hold, without fear of losing it forever. Your contact database is one of the most important communication assets you have.</p><p>Once you&#8217;ve defined the target or targets you want to reach, you need a database where you can identify whether that target actually exists, so you can make contact.</p><h2>An exercise for this week</h2><p>Open your contact database and try to answer three questions:</p><ol><li><p>How many contacts do you actually have?</p></li><li><p>How many of those are your target audience?</p></li><li><p>How many have a valid email address?</p></li></ol><p>If the answers surprise you, you&#8217;re in the right place. How to communicate with that audience will be the topic of one of the next newsletters.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Your visitors don’t just want to look]]></title><description><![CDATA[Nearly half of all museum visits are driven by social motivation. What happens when you design for belonging?]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-visitors-dont-just-want-to-look</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/your-visitors-dont-just-want-to-look</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 08 Apr 2026 11:00:46 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/c96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1294607,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;Ten people, a blend of genders and ethnicities, are seated in a casual circle within a brightly illuminated museum gallery. Tall white walls are covered with modern art photography. Summer evening, with soft ambient lighting. Business casual wear.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/192824525?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="Ten people, a blend of genders and ethnicities, are seated in a casual circle within a brightly illuminated museum gallery. Tall white walls are covered with modern art photography. Summer evening, with soft ambient lighting. Business casual wear." title="Ten people, a blend of genders and ethnicities, are seated in a casual circle within a brightly illuminated museum gallery. Tall white walls are covered with modern art photography. Summer evening, with soft ambient lighting. Business casual wear." srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FBuE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc96fb140-469a-42ff-80e3-516dec512d30_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Nearly half of all museum visits are driven by social motivation, not cultural curiosity. This comes from research by Morris Hargreaves McIntyre across institutions including the British Museum and Tate: 48% of visits originate from the desire to be with someone, not to see something.</p><p><strong>And yet most cultural institutions keep designing experiences for silent spectators. </strong>Rooms, paths, panels, audio guides. Everything built for the individual who observes. Little or nothing built for people who want to feel part of something.</p><p>I find this number important. Because it changes the underlying question: no longer &#8220;how do I improve the visit experience?&#8221; but &#8220;how do I make people feel less alone?&#8221;</p><div><hr></div><p>I&#8217;m in a lot of WhatsApp groups, and I often wonder whether it&#8217;s the right moment to write or not. I use them to share a smile, not to get into deep conversations I&#8217;d rather have face to face. But I notice that for many people, those groups are exactly that: spaces to stay connected, to feed the need to feel part of something.</p><p>With two small kids, I&#8217;m in a phase of life where private moments feel like diamonds. When I walk into a museum, I want to immerse myself in what the curator has prepared. It&#8217;s like a gift to unwrap in private, room by room.</p><p>But that&#8217;s not the case for everyone. And it probably won&#8217;t be the case for me forever either.</p><div><hr></div><p>Some institutions have already picked up on this need, with measurable results.</p><p>The Santa Cruz Museum of Art &amp; History, under Nina Simon&#8217;s direction (2011-2019), went from 17,000 to 148,000 visitors per year by transforming from a traditional museum into a community hub. Over 4,000 local artists and community groups collaborated on programming. Revenue grew from $630,000 to $2.5 million.</p><p>The Museo Egizio in Turin nearly doubled its visitors over a decade, going from roughly 570,000 to over a million. But the most interesting figure isn&#8217;t the numerical growth. It&#8217;s what they did alongside it: training migrants as cultural mediators, organizing guided tours for homeless families, running Italian language courses for young immigrants through Egyptian art. <strong>The museum became a place where you don&#8217;t just go to look, but to belong.</strong></p><div><hr></div><p>There&#8217;s a concept that connects these cases. And it&#8217;s uncomfortable for anyone directing a cultural institution.</p><p><strong>These museums accepted to give up a bit of cultural sovereignty to their audience.</strong></p><p>This doesn&#8217;t mean losing control. It means accepting that the interaction doesn&#8217;t have to be one-directional only. That there are moments when the audience isn&#8217;t there just to receive, but to contribute. To meet others who share the same interests. To feel part of a community that goes beyond the institution-visitor relationship.</p><p>The organization&#8217;s role doesn&#8217;t disappear, it changes shape: moderating, organizing, maintaining the boundaries within the cultural perimeter that holds the community together. Like in a good WhatsApp group, the value isn&#8217;t just in the content shared, but in the feeling of belonging.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>Your spaces have something that social media will never have: physical presence, the implicit rules of respect, a context that invites constructive exchange. </strong>In an era where the WHO estimates that loneliness affects 1 in 6 people globally and contributes to approximately 871,000 deaths per year, cultural spaces can become something more than what they are today.</p><p>The question isn&#8217;t whether your visitors seek social connection. The data says nearly half already do.</p><p>The question is: are you ready to design for that need?</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[What do your visitors hear when you don't give them headphones?]]></title><description><![CDATA[Sound is as much a part of your exhibition as lighting. Most museums don't design it. Here's why that matters.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/what-do-your-visitors-hear-when-you</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/what-do-your-visitors-hear-when-you</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 01 Apr 2026 11:02:53 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1135787,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A happy person wearing headphones stands among a sad crowd without headphones inside a museum.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/192074451?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A happy person wearing headphones stands among a sad crowd without headphones inside a museum." title="A happy person wearing headphones stands among a sad crowd without headphones inside a museum." srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!G4MG!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F55eb08bf-0d6c-4d13-be79-d52e943f7b7f_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Every Wednesday, in this season that&#8217;s taking its time to warm up, I go back to the same caf&#233;, greeted by the same barista, but today without the usual background music.</p><p>She was in the mood to vent. While bringing my coffee to the table, she told me she couldn&#8217;t turn on the music because a customer on a phone call had asked her to switch it off. Normally her space fills with a vintage lounge sound, perfect for easing into 8 AM. Today, silence.</p><p>I&#8217;m sitting here writing, but the mood isn&#8217;t right. I usually rely on that gentle background to slide into the creative flow that produces the reflections I share with you in this newsletter. This morning&#8217;s silence took something away.</p><p>And that got me thinking: if I notice immediately when the right background sound is missing in a caf&#233;, how much do visitors notice in your spaces?</p><p><strong>Sound is as much a part of an exhibition as lighting or the arrangement of works.</strong> Yet almost no museum designs it intentionally. As sound designer Christine Murray told the American Alliance of Museums: sound is often the last thing exhibition designers think about, something that gets tacked on at the end.</p><p>The numbers confirm this is a mistake. A 2025 study published in the Journal of the Acoustical Society of America tested different sound conditions with 23 visitors across 16 exhibitions in five American museums. The result: 58% of participants preferred a soundscape congruent with the exhibition&#8217;s content. Not generic music, not silence. Sounds designed for that specific experience. Silence, which many consider the natural default, came second at 20%.</p><p>What do your visitors hear during their visit? Even when you don&#8217;t give them headphones, what is the background sound that accompanies them?</p><p>Is it part of the exhibition design? Do you avoid it entirely so it won&#8217;t interfere with the visit? Are there natural sounds already permeating your spaces?</p><p>There&#8217;s a phenomenon that anyone managing crowded spaces knows well, even without giving it a name. A study conducted in a museum in Florence measured sound levels as visitor numbers in a single room fluctuated between 10 and 130 people. As background noise rises, people speak louder to be heard, which raises the noise further, which makes everyone speak louder still. It&#8217;s a self-reinforcing cycle, and in historic buildings where you can&#8217;t retrofit the acoustics, it becomes unbearable fast.</p><p>I&#8217;d like to suggest a simple experiment. Go listen to your spaces at two extreme moments: empty and packed. Pay attention to how pleasant or unpleasant the sounds feel. A layer of white noise or neutral ambient sound can help immerse the visitor without touching anyone&#8217;s personal music taste.</p><p>And there&#8217;s one more thing worth considering. An audio guide is not just words. Beyond narration, it can carry its own soundscape, a subtle layer of sound that sets the mood for each room or each artwork. Think of it as giving your visitor a personal sonic environment that travels with them, independent of whatever is happening in the physical space around them. <strong>A well-designed audio guide can solve the sound problem at the individual level, even when you can&#8217;t control the acoustics of the room.</strong></p><p>When I think about my own experiences, very different situations come to mind:</p><ul><li><p>Audio guides with overbearing medieval soundtracks, where the music drowned the words instead of supporting them.</p></li><li><p>Deep silence in certain rooms. I loved carving out that stillness because in some spaces it&#8217;s essential.</p></li><li><p>Unbearable chatter echoing everywhere, to the point where I couldn&#8217;t read a wall label with any concentration.</p></li></ul><p>Next time you walk into your spaces, try closing your eyes for thirty seconds. What you hear is part of your visitor experience. The question is: did you choose it, or did it just happen?</p><div><hr></div><p><em>P.S. Speaking of sound and mood: if you ever work in noisy or overly quiet environments, try Endel. It&#8217;s an app that generates adaptive soundscapes for focus or relaxation. I use it during deep focus sessions and it really makes a difference.</em></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[One Bad Detail Ruins Everything]]></title><description><![CDATA[A walking city taught me how great experiences work. Then I made a checklist.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/one-bad-detail-ruins-everything</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/one-bad-detail-ruins-everything</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 25 Mar 2026 12:02:35 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg" width="1280" height="960" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:960,&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1274073,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/191348163?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ld4i!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F10d041fd-5506-4777-8bc7-5d2b6b366f1e_1280x960.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Last week I was walking through one of the most important cities in my country. I kept asking myself how, despite the overtourism, the tourist shops everywhere, the disappearing residents, this city remains so beautiful. Every time I visit, day or night, it leaves me speechless.</p><p>Is it the stunning palaces reflected on the water? Is it the bacari serving cicchetti, those small bites similar to Spanish tapas, and spritz on every corner? Maybe it&#8217;s the endless narrow streets that give you the feeling there&#8217;s always a new area waiting to be discovered.</p><p>I&#8217;m talking about Venice, obviously.</p><p>But the question I bring home every time is this: how do you concentrate all this beauty? How do you deliver a repeated, winning series of wow moments?</p><p>Through a vast mix of senses involved. The sound of water, the sight of the palaces, the taste of cicchetti, and the right temperature on your skin make even the smell of the sea pleasant. When an experience engages multiple senses at the same time, it becomes hard to match.</p><p>And this applies to you too.</p><p>You don&#8217;t need Venice at your disposal. You need to ask yourself: how many senses are we engaging in our space? What does the visitor see, hear, touch, smell? And most importantly: are we feeding their curiosity? The curiosity to read, to see, to listen, to taste.</p><p>Every interaction is an opportunity. You can work on your spaces to make them more beautiful. You can accompany visitors with sounds that relax or voices that teach. You can offer a break with quality flavours.</p><p>But there is one principle that comes before everything else: a single negative micro-experience can ruin all the others.</p><p>You don&#8217;t need to add more experiences. You need to eliminate the ones that destroy them first.</p><h2>The sensory audit: what ruins the rest</h2><p>Here is a list of small things that, on their own, can compromise an otherwise excellent experience:</p><ul><li><p>Undrinkable coffee.</p></li><li><p>Construction noise from a nearby site.</p></li><li><p>A corridor along the route used as a chaotic storage room.</p></li><li><p>A staircase covered in mould.</p></li><li><p>A messy bookshop.</p></li><li><p>Rude front desk staff.</p></li><li><p>An audio guide that doesn&#8217;t follow the visitor route in order.</p></li><li><p>An unnatural path, unclear to follow.</p></li><li><p>Lights that are too harsh.</p></li><li><p>Difficulty reaching the entrance.</p></li><li><p>Too many panels to read.</p></li><li><p>A smelly carpet.</p></li><li><p>No drinking water available.</p></li><li><p>Dirty or broken toilets.</p></li><li><p>An e-commerce that doesn&#8217;t work perfectly.</p></li></ul><p>Everything that is clearly connected to the experience you offer, even indirectly, must be eliminated. If you can&#8217;t eliminate it, flag it. If you can&#8217;t flag it, at least acknowledge it.</p><p>The next step is simple: take this list, walk through your space, and mark how many of these you find. Not to punish yourself, but to know where to start.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The real face of a cultural organization]]></title><description><![CDATA[The people at your entrance desk matter more than your audioguide software. Most institutions haven't noticed yet.]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-real-face-of-a-cultural-organization</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-real-face-of-a-cultural-organization</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 18 Mar 2026 12:02:41 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:974545,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A smiling woman behind the checkout counter of a museum bookshop, telling a joke and making the customers in line laugh; bright indoor scene with shelves of art books and colorful souvenirs in the background&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/190598181?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A smiling woman behind the checkout counter of a museum bookshop, telling a joke and making the customers in line laugh; bright indoor scene with shelves of art books and colorful souvenirs in the background" title="A smiling woman behind the checkout counter of a museum bookshop, telling a joke and making the customers in line laugh; bright indoor scene with shelves of art books and colorful souvenirs in the background" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MOav!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F77081fec-da70-42e9-b819-63ea27b09247_2224x1664.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Last week, at one of the caf&#233;s where I like to have breakfast, I walked up to pay in a slightly low mood. The barista, who has been working there for years, caught me tapping my smartwatch on the POS terminal. She smiled and started telling me a funny story. A lady had come in the day before, coat sleeve pulled up, rotating the back of her hand over the reader with growing frustration. The barista had eventually leaned over and asked, very seriously, whether she had a chip implanted under her skin.</p><p>It made me laugh. My mood shifted in about ten seconds.</p><p>I grew up in Romagna, a region in northern Italy with a coastline that, honestly, isn&#8217;t the most beautiful in the country. Yet it became one of the most visited tourist destinations in Italy. The reason isn&#8217;t the sea. It&#8217;s the people who run the places along it. The hospitality there isn&#8217;t a policy written in a manual. It&#8217;s something people carry naturally, and it has always been the real competitive advantage.</p><p>I thought about that walking out of the caf&#233;.</p><p>When you work in a museum, a theater, or a library, you have front office staff. The ticket desk, the bookshop, the entrance. <strong>Those people are what visitors actually remember.</strong> Not the audioguide app. Not the digital ticketing system. <strong>The person who looked them in the eyes when they walked in.</strong></p><p>We are in a moment where cultural institutions are digitalizing many interactions with visitors. That trend will continue. But <strong>the human interactions that remain will become proportionally more important, not less</strong>. Being made to feel welcome, at ease, even entertained, will carry more weight precisely because it&#8217;s becoming rarer.</p><p>Here is the tension I keep thinking about.</p><p>A museum invests 20,000 euros in a new audioguide platform. Months of procurement, demos, negotiations. The institution treats it as a strategic decision, as it should be.</p><p>Then they hire the front desk staff on the lowest contract available.</p><p>The visitor spends four minutes with the audioguide and forty minutes interacting with the people in the building. The math is uncomfortable.</p><p>The staff who represent your organization every single day deserve the same strategic attention you give to your software vendors. That means understanding how they feel about their work, whether they have the tools and training to do it well, and whether they actually enjoy being there. Because that last part, you cannot fake. Visitors feel it immediately.</p><p><strong>When did you last ask your front desk team how they feel about their work?</strong></p><p>Claudio.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The value of who gives, not just what is given]]></title><description><![CDATA[What if the wellbeing museums create doesn't start with the visitor but with the people who choose to support them?]]></description><link>https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-value-of-who-gives-not-just-what</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://claudiobuda.substack.com/p/the-value-of-who-gives-not-just-what</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Claudio Buda]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 11 Mar 2026 12:03:24 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png" width="1456" height="1089" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/a1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1089,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:7017122,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;A visitor stepping out of a large contemporary art museum, face lit by a radiant smile and surrounded by an intense, luminous aura of happiness, warm late-afternoon sunlight streaming through expansive glass windows, soft golden reflections on the floor.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/png&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://claudiobuda.substack.com/i/189864325?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="A visitor stepping out of a large contemporary art museum, face lit by a radiant smile and surrounded by an intense, luminous aura of happiness, warm late-afternoon sunlight streaming through expansive glass windows, soft golden reflections on the floor." title="A visitor stepping out of a large contemporary art museum, face lit by a radiant smile and surrounded by an intense, luminous aura of happiness, warm late-afternoon sunlight streaming through expansive glass windows, soft golden reflections on the floor." srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!SKNk!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa1fc80e0-2082-473e-b62d-26ebe6f280aa_2224x1664.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg role="img" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><title></title><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>For a few years I ran a small local chapter of a blood donation association. Our job was simple to describe, harder to do well: bring people closer to voluntary donation. And <strong>every year we were evaluated on one number: how many donations we had collected</strong>.</p><p>I always thought that number told only half the story.</p><p><strong>A donation is an event. A donor is a relationship.</strong> And a donor who finds genuine wellbeing in giving, who feels part of something, who carries a sense of belonging after each visit to the donation center, has a power of contagion that no donation counter can ever measure.</p><p>That distinction stayed with me for years. And over the last few months I keep finding it again, almost identical, in cultural organizations.</p><div><hr></div><p>Museums and theatres are changing how they think about their audiences. It&#8217;s not a loud revolution, it&#8217;s more of a quiet reckoning. <strong>The old model was transactional: sell a ticket, generate a visit, count the footfall. The new one is relational: build a community, cultivate a connection, generate value over time.</strong></p><p>This shift isn&#8217;t coming from visitor demand. It&#8217;s coming from inside the organizations themselves, as they begin to understand where their real value lives.</p><p>The problem, though, is that this value is hard to bring to the table.</p><p>When a museum sits down with a public authority or a funder, the accepted language is that of classic numbers: attendance figures, economic impact on the territory, jobs generated, tourism spending. Those numbers are real and useful. But they don&#8217;t tell you what actually happens inside a person after two hours spent in a museum.</p><div><hr></div><p>Recently I came across a report published by Museums as Progress, put together by Dr. John H. Falk alongside three American museum directors. The document tackles exactly this problem: how to articulate museum value with new arguments, ones capable of reaching stakeholders for whom the traditional economic case no longer lands.</p><p>The central finding from Falk&#8217;s research is that a museum visit produces measurable benefits to visitors&#8217; personal, intellectual, social, and physical wellbeing, with a perceived economic value of around $500 per visit. This isn&#8217;t an invented number, it&#8217;s calculated based on what people would pay to achieve equivalent outcomes through other means.</p><p>What strikes me most about the report isn&#8217;t the figure itself, but how the three directors pressure-tested it in real conversations: with government officials, with foundations, with major donors. <strong>What they learned: that economic data and wellbeing data work better kept separate, not combined.</strong> That the relationship built over years is worth as much as the new argument. That wellbeing has to be lived internally before it can be communicated outward.</p><div><hr></div><p>So much for the report. Now my contribution.</p><p>There&#8217;s something the document doesn&#8217;t explore, and that my years with blood donors taught me very directly: wellbeing doesn&#8217;t only come from receiving a cultural experience. It can also come from the act of contributing itself.</p><p>Anyone who has ever donated blood knows what I mean. That sense of wellbeing you feel walking out of the donation center has nothing to do with what you received. It&#8217;s about what you gave. You contributed to something larger than yourself, and your body and mind both know it.</p><p>One thing worth saying clearly here: this is not an argument for paying cultural workers less because their job is &#8220;meaningful&#8221;. The wellbeing that comes from contributing is an added value, not a substitute for fair economic recognition. Those are completely different conversations.</p><p>Back to the question: why don&#8217;t we apply the same logic to museums? <strong>Why do we measure wellbeing only from the visitor&#8217;s side and not from that of the supporter, the volunteer, the cultural donor?</strong></p><p>If the value of a visit can be measured at $500 in perceived wellbeing, what is the wellbeing worth of the person who made that visit possible by supporting the museum over time?</p><p>This feels to me like the unexplored frontier of the cultural value conversation. Not just how much institutions give to the community, but how much the community gains from the very act of sustaining its own institutions.</p><div><hr></div><p>I don&#8217;t have a definitive answer yet. But I&#8217;m convinced that the shift from transactional to relational in museums is incomplete until it includes this dimension too: the wellbeing of who gives, not just of who visits.</p><p>If you work in a museum and you&#8217;re looking for new arguments to explain your value to funders, the Museums as Progress report, published March 2026, is a serious starting point.</p><p>Claudio.</p>]]></content:encoded></item></channel></rss>